#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065343 :tutorial/content "When simplifying an algebraic expression involving square roots, it's useful to remember that\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x^2} = \\mid x \\mid\n$$\n\nIt's tempting to think that $\\sqrt{x^2}$ is equivalent to $x$, but that's not true because $\\sqrt{x^2}$ is always non-negative, even if $x$ is negative.\n\nTo illustrate, let's evaluate $\\sqrt{x^2}$ for a couple of different values of $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{4^{2}} &= \\sqrt{16} = 4 \\\\\n\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}} &= \\sqrt{16} = 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn both cases, using absolute value gives the correct result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{4^{2}} = & 4 & &= 4 & ✓ \\\\ \\sqrt{(-4)^{2}} = & -4 & |= 4 & ✓\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4e09a82d-1598-56ed-9888-9a1bfa380f55" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1604 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
